PDV-2.1.2.jar in this directory is PDV (Proteomics Data Viewer), version 2.1.2, by its upstream authors. It is not part of TARSUS and is not covered by the TARSUS MIT license.

License: GNU General Public License v3.0 (GPL-3.0). Full text: https://www.gnu.org/licenses/gpl-3.0.txt

Upstream project and source code: https://github.com/wenbostar/PDV

Source for this version: upstream has no 2.1.2 release or tag. The 2.1.2 source is the upstream tree from commit dacf4b6c0f20027a9707d756d4fbea2542599b4e ("Bump to 2.1.2", 2025-01-20), which sets the version to 2.1.2; the version stays 2.1.2 until commit c0b60ce0f3 ("Bump to 2.1.3", 2025-04-07). The exact upstream commit this JAR was built from is not recorded.

SHA256 of PDV-2.1.2.jar: 8757fbdd053949edc421a0de5b4fc8b37f46b8518646d69b90bd16055bb4adf1 (checked by `make pdv`).

Why 2.1.2: it is the PDV version the reference pipeline used, so TARSUS runs the same version (src/tarsus/env.py loads tools/PDV-2.1.2/PDV-2.1.2.jar). Because there is no public 2.1.2 download, the JAR is committed here.

Why lib/ and resources/ come from PDV 2.2.3: upstream release zips ship the JAR with lib/ and resources/ beside it, and the JAR's manifest loads its dependencies from lib/ next to the JAR. Neither directory is committed. `make pdv` downloads the public PDV 2.2.3 release (https://github.com/wenbostar/PDV/releases/download/v2.2.3/PDV-2.2.3.zip, SHA256 pinned in the Makefile), extracts it to tools/PDV-2.2.3/, and links tools/PDV-2.1.2/lib and tools/PDV-2.1.2/resources to it. This is the layout TARSUS runs with on its development cluster, and `make test-pdv-full` checks that PDV draws figures with it.
